images.annotation:
{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing economic activity trends from November 2020 to March 2024. The chart includes three lines representing total economic activity, economic activity excluding agriculture, and agricultural economic activity. The y-axis on the left measures total and non-agricultural economic activity, while the y-axis on the right measures agricultural economic activity. The chart indicates fluctuations in economic activity over the specified period, with notable peaks and troughs."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the evolution of exchange rate expectations for the Argentine Peso (ARS) against the US Dollar (US$) from January 2024 to January 2025. The chart includes three lines representing different future contract dates: end-December 2023, end-February 2024, and end-May 2024. The exchange rate is shown on the vertical axis, ranging from 800 to 2000, while the horizontal axis represents the months from January 2024 to January 2025. The chart indicates a steady increase in the exchange rate over time, with the end-December 2023 line showing the highest rate, followed by end-February 2024 and end-May 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the real revenue and spending growth in percentage, year-over-year, cumulative since December 2023. The chart includes categories such as Revenue, Primary spending, Capital, Current transfers provinces, Pensions, Subsidies, Wages, and Social transfers. The bars indicate a decline in all categories, with Capital showing the most significant drop, followed by Current transfers provinces and Pensions."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the Peso Debt Service in ARS billions from 2024 to 2028. The chart is divided into Pre-Exchange and Post-Exchange categories. Pre-Exchange is represented by light blue bars, and Post-Exchange is represented by dark blue bars. The chart indicates a significant amount of debt service in 2024, primarily in the Pre-Exchange category. In 2025, there is a notable increase in Post-Exchange debt service, while Pre-Exchange debt service decreases. From 2026 to 2028, Post-Exchange debt service remains relatively stable, while Pre-Exchange debt service is minimal or non-existent."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the factors of monetary base growth in trillions of AR$ from December 11 to May 23. The chart includes categories such as total, interest payments, FX purchases, net credit to government, peso-denominated securities and other BCRA liabilities, and BOPREAL. The total growth is slightly positive, with significant contributions from interest payments and FX purchases, while net credit to government and peso-denominated securities show negative values."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "This line chart illustrates the relationship between the policy rate and core inflation from November 2022 to May 2024. It includes four data series: real interest rate (ex-post), real interest rate (ex-ante), policy rate (effective terms), and core inflation. The chart shows fluctuations in these rates over time, with notable peaks and troughs. The real interest rate (ex-post) and (ex-ante) are depicted in gray and yellow bars, respectively, while the policy rate (effective terms) and core inflation are shown in light blue and dark blue lines. The data sources are BCRA, INDEC, and Fund staff estimates."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the credit to the private sector in terms of FX and Peso from December 2022 to April 2024. The chart indicates the percentage change on a month-to-month basis, with a 3-month moving average. The FX line shows a significant increase starting around the end of 2023, peaking in early 2024, while the Peso line shows a decline over the same period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the net international reserves in billions of US dollars from January 2023 to June 2024. The chart compares the target reserves (as per the 7th review) with the actual reserves. The target reserves remain constant at certain levels and then increase, while the actual reserves fluctuate significantly, initially decreasing sharply, then recovering and surpassing the target by mid-2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the primary balance in billions of AR$, cumulative floor since the start of the year. The chart compares the actual primary balance (light blue line) with the PB target (7th review) (dark blue line) from January 2023 to July 2024. The actual primary balance starts above the target but drops significantly below it around November 2023, then rises sharply above the target by mid-2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing monetary financing in billions of AR$, with cumulative ceiling since the start of the year. The chart compares actual values (in light blue) to target values (in dark blue) as of the 7th review. The x-axis represents the timeline from January 2023 to July 2024, while the y-axis represents the amount in billions of AR$. The actual values show significant fluctuations, particularly a sharp decline around early 2024, while the target values remain relatively stable with minor increases."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the real minimum pension benefits in Argentina from January 2021 to July 2028, measured in thousands of AR$ at constant prices. The chart includes four lines representing different scenarios: "
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "This scatter plot shows the maturity and yields of ARS (CER) Debt Issuances. The x-axis represents the maturity in days, ranging from 0 to 1500 days, and the y-axis represents the yields in percent, ranging from -120% to 60%. The data points are color-coded: light blue for the period of September to November 2023 and dark blue for the period of December 2023 to May 2024. The chart indicates a general trend where yields vary significantly with different maturities, with some extreme negative yields observed at shorter maturities."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the private and official debt maturity profile from 2024 to 2028, measured in billions of US dollars. The chart is divided into different categories of debt: FX Bonds, Peso Bonds, IMF, Bopreal, and Other Official. Each year is represented by a stacked bar, with each segment of the bar indicating the amount of debt in each category for that year."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar and diamond chart showing the cumulative difference in inflation rates for various categories relative to the GBA headline inflation since December 2019. The data is compared between April 2024 and November 2023. Categories include Purchase of Vehicle, Housing Maintenance & Repair, Clothing & Accessories, Books & Stationery, Restaurants & Hotels, Footwear, Medical Products & Appliances, Personal Care, Audiovisual, Fuels & Lubricants, Home Appliances & Equipment, Education, Public Transportation, Electricity & Gas, Communication, and Rent for Housing. The chart indicates significant variations in inflation rates across different categories, with some categories experiencing higher inflation in April 2024 compared to November 2023, and others showing a decrease."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the breakdown of real electricity bills from January to April 2024, measured in constant January 2024 Argentine pesos (AR$). The chart is divided into four categories: Generation, Distribution (VAD), Tax (24.7%), and Subsidy. Each bar represents a different time period (N1, N2, N3) within January and February to April. The chart also includes a secondary axis on the right-hand side indicating the percentage of minimum salary. The data sources are MECON and Fund staff calculations."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the trade balance and real GDP level from 2023Q1 to 2025Q4. The trade balance is represented in billions of US dollars and the GDP is represented in billions of AR$, seasonally adjusted. The chart shows two lines: one for the trade balance (light blue) and one for GDP (dark blue). The trade balance fluctuates around the zero line, while the GDP shows a general upward trend over the period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the Real Wage Index from January 2017 to January 2024. The chart includes four lines representing different sectors: Formal sector (private), Formal sector (public), Formal sector (combined), and Informal sector. The index starts at 100 in January 2017 and shows a general decline over the period, with noticeable drops around mid-2020 and early 2023. The informal sector shows the steepest decline, while the formal sectors show a more gradual decline."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the AUH Benefits in thousands of AR$, constant December 2023 prices, from January 2018 to April 2024. The chart indicates fluctuations in the benefits over time, with a notable sharp decline around late 2023 followed by a significant increase in early 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bubble chart showing provincial imbalances in public sector dependence. The x-axis represents public provincial employment as a percent of formal employment, and the y-axis represents own-source revenues as a percent of total revenues. Each bubble represents a different province, with the size of the bubble indicating the population size of the province. The chart highlights the varying degrees of public sector dependence across different provinces."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the primary fiscal balance as a percent of GDP, comparing successful and not successful cases over a range of values from -5 to 5."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the 'Ex-ante' Real Interest Rate in percent for two categories: Successful and Not Successful. The x-axis ranges from -5 to 5, and the y-axis ranges from -1,200 to 400. The chart illustrates how the interest rates for both categories fluctuate over the given range, with the Successful category showing a more volatile pattern compared to the Not Successful category."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the growth of money balances in percent for two groups: Successful and Not Successful. The x-axis represents a range of values from -5 to 5, while the y-axis represents the percentage growth, ranging from 0 to 1,800. The chart indicates that the Successful group experienced significant growth around the -3 to 0 range, peaking at around 1,400 percent, while the Not Successful group had a notable peak at 0, reaching approximately 1,200 percent, but then declined sharply."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the real effective exchange rate over a period of time, with two lines representing successful and unsuccessful stabilization episodes. The x-axis represents the time period, while the y-axis represents the exchange rate index. The chart indicates that successful stabilization episodes tend to have a more stable and lower exchange rate compared to unsuccessful ones."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the change in international reserves as a percent of GDP for successful and unsuccessful cases. The x-axis represents the percent of GDP, ranging from -5 to 5, and the y-axis represents the change in international reserves. The chart includes two lines: one for successful cases in dark blue and another for unsuccessful cases in light blue. The successful line generally trends upward, while the unsuccessful line shows more volatility and fluctuates more significantly."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the persistence of inflation in various countries at different percentiles (40 percent, 20 percent, 10 percent) and the starting inflation rate. The x-axis lists countries and the year of the data, while the y-axis on the left measures the number of months to reach the inflation threshold. The y-axis on the right measures the starting inflation rate. The chart indicates that Israel ('85) and Mexico ('87) had the highest starting inflation rates and the longest time to reach the inflation threshold, while Argentina ('91) and Peru ('93) had lower starting inflation rates and shorter times to reach the threshold."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart depicting the International Investment Position (IIP) in billions of US dollars from 2016Q1 to 2023Q3. The chart shows the overall NIIP, public NIIP, and private NIIP. The overall NIIP and private NIIP are represented by light blue and red lines, respectively, while the public NIIP is represented by a dark blue line. The overall NIIP and private NIIP show an upward trend, peaking around 2020Q1 and then slightly declining. The public NIIP shows a downward trend throughout the period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the current account outstanding FX backlog from 2020 to April 2024, measured in billions of US dollars. The chart is divided into two categories: importers' debt (in light blue) and dividend backlog (in dark blue). The average import financing level is indicated by a dashed line at approximately 30 billion US dollars. The chart shows an increasing trend in the total backlog, with a significant rise in 2023 and April 2024, indicating growing importers' debt and dividend backlog over the years."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing economic activity from December 2019 to March 2024. The chart includes two lines: one for non-agricultural economic activity (light blue) and one for overall economic activity (dark blue). The y-axis represents the index value with a base of 100 set in January 2020, and the x-axis represents the timeline. The chart shows a significant dip in economic activity around March 2020, likely due to the COVID-19 pandemic, followed by a recovery and fluctuations over the subsequent years."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the Real Wage Index from February 2020 to February 2024, with October 2016 as the baseline (index value of 100). The chart includes four lines representing different employment categories: All, Private formal, Private informal, and Public formal. The index values for all categories show a general decline over the period, with the Private informal category experiencing the steepest decline. The chart highlights the impact of various economic factors on real wages across different employment sectors over time."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the volume of imports and exports from September 2017 to March 2024, indexed to March 2017 = 100. The chart includes two lines: one for imports (in light blue) and one for exports (in dark blue). The data shows fluctuations in both imports and exports over the period, with notable peaks and troughs. Exports generally have higher values compared to imports, with significant peaks around early 2021 and early 2023."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the contributions of various components to real GDP growth on a quarterly basis from Q1 2022 to Q4 2023. The components include Private Consumption, Public Consumption, Investment, Exports, Imports, and Others. Each bar is segmented to show the contribution of each component, with positive contributions above the horizontal axis and negative contributions below it. The chart indicates fluctuations in GDP growth contributions over the specified period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar and line chart showing the unemployment and employment rates in 31 urban centers from Q4 2017 to Q4 2023. The blue bars represent the unemployment rate, while the dark blue line represents the employment rate. The chart indicates a significant spike in unemployment during 2020, likely due to the COVID-19 pandemic, followed by a gradual decline. The employment rate shows an inverse relationship, dropping sharply during the same period and then gradually increasing."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart depicting the Goods Trade Balance from August 2017 to April 2024. The chart shows three data series: Trade Balance (grey bars), Exports (light blue line), and Imports (dark blue line). The y-axis on the left represents the trade balance in billions of US dollars, while the y-axis on the right represents the exports and imports in billions of US dollars. The chart indicates fluctuations in trade balance, exports, and imports over the specified period, with notable peaks and troughs."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the inflation components (Core and Headline) from February 2022 to April 2024. The y-axis represents the percentage change month-over-month, ranging from 0 to 30 percent. The x-axis represents the timeline from February 2022 to April 2024. The chart shows two lines: one for Core inflation and one for Headline inflation. Both lines exhibit fluctuations, with notable peaks around October 2023 and a subsequent decline towards April 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the rate of crawl and inflation from February 2022 to April 2024. The chart uses two colors to differentiate between the rate of crawl (light blue) and inflation (dark blue). The y-axis represents the percentage (m/m), and the x-axis represents the months and years. The chart shows a significant spike in the rate of crawl in October 2023, reaching 124%, while inflation also shows notable increases during the same period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the trend of Real BCRA Liabilities from February 2022 to April 2024. The index starts at 100 in January 2022 and shows a general downward trend, indicating a decrease in real liabilities over the period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing inflation expectations from September 2023 to March 2026. The chart is divided into three sections: End-2024, End-2025, and End-2026. Each section shows data points for both headline and core inflation expectations. Headline inflation is represented by blue bars, while core inflation is represented by black dots. The y-axis represents the percentage change year-over-year, ranging from 0 to 300 percent. The chart indicates higher inflation expectations at the end of 2024, with a noticeable decline in expectations for the end of 2025 and 2026."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the policy rate and inflation over time, with three different data series: LELIQ (solid line), Pases ON (dashed line), and Inflation (solid blue line). The x-axis represents the timeline from August to May, and the y-axis represents the percentage (m/m). The chart illustrates fluctuations in the policy rates and inflation, with notable peaks and declines in the data series."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the real money balance from February 2022 to April 2024. The chart includes three lines representing the Monetary Base, M2, and M3, with the index set to 100 in January 2022. The Monetary Base line shows a significant decline from around 100 in February 2022 to below 40 in April 2024. The M2 line also shows a decline but at a slower rate, starting around 100 and dropping to around 50 by April 2024. The M3 line follows a similar trend to M2 but with more fluctuations, starting around 100 and ending slightly above 40 in April 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the cumulative FX intervention in the official market in billions of US dollars from early July to early May. The chart indicates a significant increase in intervention starting around early November, with a steep upward trend continuing through the end of the period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the EMBIG Sovereign Spread in basis points for Emerging markets and Argentina from July 2023 to May 2024. The chart has two lines: one for Emerging markets (dark blue) and one for Argentina (light blue). The y-axis on the left measures the spread in basis points, ranging from 250 to 500, while the y-axis on the right measures another scale ranging from 1100 to 3100. The x-axis represents the timeline from July 2023 to May 2024. The chart shows a general downward trend in the spreads for both Emerging markets and Argentina, with some fluctuations. Notably, Argentina's spread is consistently higher than that of the Emerging markets throughout the period."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the exchange rate gaps (in percent, relative to the official exchange rate) over time, from August 4 to May 15. The chart includes two data series: CCL gap (in dark blue) and BLUE gap (in light blue). The y-axis represents the percentage gap, ranging from 0 to 250, while the x-axis represents the timeline. The chart indicates significant fluctuations in exchange rate gaps, with notable peaks in October and December, followed by a sharp decline in December and relatively stable values from January to May."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "Line chart showing the evolution of market devaluation expectations based on ROFEX future contracts, with data points for end-December, end-February, and end-May. The chart tracks the percentage change month-over-month from January 2024 to December 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the official exchange rate (ER) and the real effective exchange rate (REER) of TAR$/US$ from August 5 to May 31. The chart indicates a significant change in the official exchange rate around December, where it sharply increases and then stabilizes at a higher level. The REER shows a sharp decline around the same period and continues to decrease gradually thereafter."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the relationship between the policy rate, core inflation, and real interest rate (ex-post) from June 2022 to April 2024. The chart includes three data series: real interest rate (ex-post) in grey, policy rate (monthly effective terms) in dark blue, and core inflation in light blue. The y-axis represents the percentage (m/m), ranging from -20 to 40, while the x-axis represents the timeline from June 2022 to April 2024. The chart indicates fluctuations in these economic indicators over the specified period, with notable peaks and troughs in core inflation and policy rate, and relatively stable real interest rates."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the overall deficit as a cumulative percent of GDP for the years 2021, 2022, 2023, and 2024. The chart indicates a progressive increase in the deficit over the months, with each year's data represented by a different shade of blue. The deficit starts near zero in January and rises steadily, peaking in December, with 2024 showing the highest deficit."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the Treasury Deposits at Central Bank in billions of AR$ from March 2022 to March 2024. The chart indicates significant fluctuations in deposits over the period, with notable peaks in August 2023 and January 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the cumulative BCRA bond purchases net of Treasury buyback in billions of AR$, from June 2022 to March 2024. The chart indicates a significant increase in bond purchases over time, with notable fluctuations and peaks, particularly around late 2023 and early 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the year-over-year percentage change in real revenues and real primary spending from January 2023 to April 2024. The chart uses two colors to differentiate between real revenue (dark blue) and real primary spending (light blue). The y-axis ranges from -50% to 40%, and the x-axis lists months from January 2023 to April 2024. The chart indicates fluctuations in both real revenue and real primary spending, with notable declines in both metrics during certain months, particularly in early 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar and line chart showing the cumulative monetary financing and domestic debt service in billions of ARS since January 2022. The chart indicates a significant increase in both monetary financing and domestic debt service over time, with a notable spike in monetary financing around mid-2023."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "Line chart showing the weighted average life of inflation-linked instruments in months from July 2022 to January 2024. The average for 2023 is marked at 7.00 months, with notable fluctuations throughout the period, including a significant spike in January 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar and line chart showing the liquidity in domestic and foreign currency as a percent of deposits from August 2018 to February 2024. The chart includes three data series: BCRA papers (light blue bars), current account in BCRA (dark blue bars), and USD reserves (black dashed line). The y-axis represents the percentage of deposits, ranging from 0 to 140 percent, while the x-axis represents the timeline from August 2018 to February 2024. The chart indicates an overall increasing trend in liquidity over the period, with notable fluctuations in USD reserves."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A chart showing the trends in lending and deposit rates from March 2018 to March 2024. The chart includes three data series: the lending-deposit rate spread (in light blue bars), the lending rate to individuals (in a dashed blue line), and deposit rates (in a solid black line). The y-axis on the left represents the lending-deposit rate spread and deposit rates in percentage, while the y-axis on the right represents the lending rate to individuals in percentage. The chart shows significant fluctuations in all three rates over the period, with notable peaks and troughs. The lending-deposit rate spread and lending rate to individuals show a sharp increase around late 2022 to early 2023, followed by a decline. Deposit rates also show a general upward trend with some fluctuations."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the distribution of assets by type as a percentage of total assets from December 2019 to February 2024. The chart categorizes assets into four types: Claims on the BCRA, Government securities, Private sector credit, and Others. Each bar represents a different time point, showing how the composition of assets has changed over time."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar and line chart showing the trends of Private Sector Non-Performing Loans (NPLs) and Capital Adequacy Ratios (CARs) from August 2018 to February 2024. The chart uses two y-axes: the left y-axis for NPLs (ranging from 0 to 10 percent) and the right y-axis for CARs (ranging from 0 to 40 percent). The x-axis represents the timeline from August 2018 to February 2024. The blue bars represent the Capital Adequacy Ratios, which show a general upward trend over the period, indicating an increase in capital adequacy. The black line represents Non-Performing Loans, which fluctuate over the period, showing peaks and troughs. Notably, NPLs peaked around February 2020 and February 2021, while CARs have consistently increased, especially from mid-2022 onwards. The chart highlights the inverse relationship between NPLs and CARs, where higher capital adequacy is associated with lower non-performing loans."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the trends in private sector deposits in real terms from August 2018 to February 2024. The chart includes two lines: one for time deposits (dashed line) and one for sight deposits (solid line). The y-axis represents the index value with January 2018 set as 100, and the x-axis represents the timeline. The chart shows fluctuations in both types of deposits over the years, with notable peaks and troughs. Time deposits show a significant drop towards the end of the period, while sight deposits also decline but to a lesser extent."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "This chart shows the real private sector credit in domestic currency from August 2018 to February 2024. The chart includes two data series: month-over-month (m/m) changes represented by light blue bars and year-over-year (y/y) changes represented by a dark blue line. The y-axis on the left measures the percentage change m/m, ranging from -20% to 20%, while the y-axis on the right measures the percentage change y/y, ranging from -50% to 40%. The chart indicates significant fluctuations in private sector credit, with notable peaks and troughs. For instance, there is a sharp decline in credit around early 2020, followed by a rapid recovery and subsequent fluctuations. The most recent data shows a steep decline in credit, reaching a low point around February 2024."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line chart showing the 10-year fanchart as a percentage of GDP from 2019 to 2033. The chart includes different shaded areas representing confidence intervals (5-25 pct, 25-50 pct, 50-75 pct, 75-95 pct) and a red line indicating the actual values. The chart shows a peak around 2023, followed by a decline and stabilization in the subsequent years."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A stacked area chart showing the projection of financial data in both foreign and local currency from 2014 to 2032. The chart indicates fluctuations in both currencies, with a notable peak around 2022-2024, followed by a decline. The foreign currency is represented in light blue, while the local currency is represented in dark blue."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A stacked area chart showing the distribution of different types of creditors from 2017 to 2023. The chart includes external private creditors, external official creditors, domestic other creditors, domestic commercial banks, and domestic central bank. The data indicates an increasing trend in the total amount of credit over the years, with external private creditors and external official creditors being the most significant contributors."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A pie chart showing the distribution of different types of law. The chart is divided into three segments: Domestic law (blue), International law (red), and Multilateral law (yellow)."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the projected marketable and nonmarketable debt from 2023 to 2029. The chart indicates a decreasing trend in both types of debt over the years, with marketable debt represented in blue and nonmarketable debt in yellow."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the projected distribution of residual maturity of some financial instruments over the years 2023, 2025, 2027, and 2029. The chart is segmented into three categories: <= 1 year (red), 1-5 years (yellow), and > 5 years (blue). The total number of instruments decreases over time, with a notable decrease in the <= 1 year category and a relatively stable number in the > 5 years category."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart comparing various economic factors over the past 5 years and projecting into the next 5 years. The chart includes primary deficit, real interest rate, real GDP growth, exchange rate depreciation, and residual. It also shows the change in public sector debt as a purple dot. The past 5 years show a significant primary deficit and residual, with a notable change in public sector debt. The next 5 years project a reduction in these factors, with a more balanced distribution among the components."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A histogram showing the distribution of 3-year debt reduction across different countries or entities. The x-axis represents the debt reduction in percentage points of GDP, while the y-axis shows the frequency of occurrences. The chart highlights that a 3-year debt reduction above the 75th percentile is 5.9 percentage points of GDP. Key elements include blue bars for the distribution, a red diamond marking the 3-year reduction, and a red triangle indicating the maximum 3-year reduction."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line and bar chart showing the impact of different fiscal multipliers on GDP from 2019 to 2026. The chart includes four lines representing different multipliers (Baseline, Multiplier=0.5, Multiplier=1, Multiplier=1.5) and bars indicating fiscal adjustments. The x-axis represents the years, while the y-axis on the left shows the percentage change and the y-axis on the right shows the percentage points of GDP."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A chart showing the projected changes in domestic real rates and spreads for different investment terms (<1 year, 1-5 years, 5+ years) from 2024 to 2029. The chart includes a line graph for domestic real rates and a stacked bar graph for the different investment terms, with each term represented by a different color."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A histogram showing the distribution of some data with a 3-year adjustment. The chart includes a percentile rank of 91, indicating that the 3-year adjustment is above the 75th percentile, contributing 2 percentage points to GDP. The maximum 3-year adjustment is marked with a red triangle."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A line and bar chart showing economic growth and output gap from 2013 to 2029. The chart includes multiple lines representing different growth metrics and a bar series representing the output gap. The y-axis on the left measures growth percentages, while the y-axis on the right measures the output gap percentages. The chart highlights fluctuations in growth rates and output gaps over the years, with notable dips and peaks, particularly around 2021."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar and line chart showing the percent of GDP over time from 2014 to 2032, with a projection starting from around 2024. The chart includes various colored bars and a blue line indicating trends and fluctuations in the data."
}

{
  "type": "chart",
  "description": "A bar chart showing the cumulative impact of various economic factors on public debt. The chart includes primary deficit, real interest rate, real GDP growth, exchange rate depreciation, other flows, residual plus relative inflation, and change in public debt. Each factor is represented by a different color and the values are plotted along the vertical axis, ranging from -250 to 150."
}

